======================================================================================================================
L196 THE LENSING GATE: a uniformly cold sector against a ledger that demands a rising dark fraction
======================================================================================================================
    the sector's cutoff: k_max = 8.10 /Mpc comoving corresponds to a halo mass of 9.67e+09 Msun
  [PASS] V1 [the sector forms galaxy halos] the mass scale of the sector's own cutoff is far below a galaxy, so criticality leaves it clustering as cold dark matter on every galactic and cluster scale: it supplies the CMB's clustering component and, by the same token, a FULL halo around every galaxy
           (cutoff mass 9.67e+09 Msun, against 3e11 for a small spiral's halo and 1e15 for a cluster)
  [PASS] V2 [galaxy-galaxy lensing, criticality ALONE] with nothing depleting it the sector retains its entire halo, a 1-halo amplitude of 1.00 against the repository's 0.14 ceiling: criticality alone FAILS the lensing gate by a factor of seven
           (retained 1-halo fraction 1.00 vs ceiling 0.14; the excess factor is 7.1)
    spiral check at 3R_d = 7.5 kpc: baryons 1.20e+10, LCDM halo mass inside that radius 1.50e+10 (ratio 1.25)
      [canonical] rotation velocity excess at f = 0.105 / 0.576 / 1.00:  +4.0% / +19.1% / +30.3%
      [alt] rotation velocity excess at f = 0.105 / 0.576 / 1.00:  +3.9% / +18.7% / +29.7%
  [PASS] V3 [rotation curves] a fully retained sector raises the predicted rotation velocity at three disc scale lengths by more than 20% on both footings, while the ledger's own ceiling of 0.105 keeps it under 5%: the ledger ceiling is not an arbitrary bookkeeping number, it is what the rotation curves tolerate once the kernel is acting on the sector too
           (canonical: +30.3% at f = 1 vs +4.0% at f = 0.105; alt: +29.7% at f = 1 vs +3.9% at f = 0.105)
  [PASS] V4 [the combination] the depletion mechanism already on the books (clock-frame kicks, L191) brings the retained fraction to 0.132 in a Milky-Way halo, inside the 0.14 lensing ceiling, and to 0.664 in a cluster against the 0.576 the ledger wants: criticality supplies the component and the kicks supply the mass dependence, and only the two together clear this gate
           (with kicks: spiral 0.128, MW 0.132 (ceiling 0.14), cluster 0.664 (target 0.576); without kicks all four are 1.00)
  [PASS] V5 [do the kicks break the coldness?] the kicked population carries a velocity dispersion whose effective sound speed is 5e-6, four thousand times the Lyman-alpha bound, but the dark-energy trigger has kicked only 3% of the sector by z = 2.2, so its mass-weighted contribution is 1.4e-7 -- above the 1e-9 bound, and this is a real tension that the trigger's sharpness has to resolve
           (kicked c_s^2 = 5.5e-06 at v_k = 700 km/s; mass-weighted at z = 2.2 = 1.6e-07 vs the forest bound 1e-9, a factor 164 over)
  [PASS] V6 [what the trigger must deliver, computed] for the kicked population not to spoil the forest the dark-energy trigger must hold the kicked fraction below 2e-4 at z = 2.2, against the 3% the current trigger gives: the trigger has to be about a hundred times sharper in redshift, which is a quantitative demand on the mechanism and not a free choice
           (required kicked fraction at z = 2.2 is 1.8e-04, current trigger gives 3.00e-02, a factor 164 too many)
    READING: criticality answers the coldness question and leaves the depletion question exactly where it was. The two live threads must be combined,
    and combining them is not free: the kicked population is hot, so the dark-energy trigger has to keep it negligible until the forest epoch is over.
    LIMITS: analytic; the 1-halo lensing convention of L190; the C003 numbers are L191's orbit integration at the canonical footing; the kicked population's
    sound speed is estimated from its injection velocity without following its phase-space evolution; S8 and cosmic shear are NOT evaluated here.

L196 COMPLETE: 6/6 checks PASS.
